    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. the Z-score part is a bit new to me though. can you walk me through how to actually calculate the standard deviation if my lead times are all over the place? like, if one shipment takes 14 days and the next takes 30, how do i factor that unpredictability into the safety stock so i don't run out?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! To calculate the standard deviation of lead time demand, you'll first need to gather data on different lead times for the past periods. Take all the lead times that have occurred until now, add them up, and divide by the number of observations to get an average lead time. Once you have the average lead time, calculate the daily demand rate (quantity divided by lead time) for each period. Next, subtract the daily demand from the respective demand figures at each lead time, square the result, and find the average of those squared differences. Finally, take the square root of that average to get the standard deviation of lead time demand. Don’t forget to adjust for any systematic deviations that may be influencing your data. This approach will help you quantify the variability and incorporate it into your safety stock equation more accurately.",
